Extends the AI-upscaled sprite-override system (previously only used for
inventory item icons - see the underlying Screen::queueRoseTattooHiresSprite()/
loadRoseTattooHiresCursorOverride() plumbing that widget_inventory.cpp
already relies on) to also cover the live scene's walking characters
(Holmes, Watson, NPCs, disguise costume-swap sprites) and bg-shape objects,
drawn from TattooScene::drawAllShapes() - previously these always rendered
as blocky nearest-neighbor upscales of the original 8-bit VGS art, even
with a fully AI-upscaled hires background/cursor/map/inventory already in
place.

Implementation:

- Screen::queueRoseTattooHiresSprite()'s per-pixel blit/scale/flip logic is
  factored out into a new shared Screen::blitRoseTattooHiresSpriteOverride()
  helper that writes into a caller-supplied ManagedSurface layer, and grows
  two new parameters queueRoseTattooHiresSprite() didn't need before:
  horizFlip and scaleVal (mirroring the same-named arguments
  BaseSurface::SHtransBlitFrom(const ImageFrame &, ...) already takes) -
  needed because walking characters can be perspective-scaled and/or
  mirrored, unlike the fixed-size inventory icons the original function was
  written for. The nativeFrame parameter is new too, needed to replicate
  SHtransBlitFrom()'s own per-frame offset handling
  (sDrawXOffset()/sDrawYOffset()) so the override lines up pixel-for-pixel
  with where the native (fallback) blit would have drawn.

- A brand new Screen::queueRoseTattooHiresSceneSprite() targets a separate
  _roseTattooHiresSceneSpriteLayer, kept on its own lifecycle from the
  existing UI sprite layer: UI widgets (inventory) only re-queue a sprite
  when actually redrawn and rely on callers to erase stale entries when
  they move/hide, but every character/object in the scene can move or
  appear/disappear on essentially every frame, and drawAllShapes() has no
  notion of a modal widget's "erase on hide" bookkeeping. So this layer is
  instead fully cleared and freshly re-populated every single time
  drawAllShapes() runs (Screen::clearRoseTattooHiresSceneSpriteLayer()),
  and blended on top of the composite in Screen::update() right before the
  UI sprite layer (Screen::blendRoseTattooHiresSceneSpriteLayer()), so
  scene sprites render underneath tooltips/inventory icons.

- ImageFile::indexOf(const ImageFrame *) is added to recover a frame's
  index (matching upscale_rosetattoo_sprites.py's "frame_NNN" numbering)
  from an already-selected ImageFrame pointer - the only thing
  Object::_imageFrame/TattooPerson::_imageFrame give scene/drawing code,
  unlike the inventory code's own explicit frame index of 0.

- tattoo_scene.cpp adds resolveRoseTattooHiresSpriteOverride() (derives the
  override resource name + frame index from an Object's/Person's current
  ImageFrame + owning ImageFile) and two small wrappers,
  queueRoseTattooHiresObjectOverride()/queueRoseTattooHiresPersonOverride(),
  wired into all 6 relevant draw call sites in drawAllShapes(): the
  BEHIND/FORWARD bg-shape loops, the y-ordered bg-shape draw, and both
  y-ordered person draw branches (SCALE_THRESHOLD and perspective-scaled).
  _activeCAnim (canimations) sites are deliberately left untouched - it's a
  StreamingImageFile with different lifetime/streaming semantics, out of
  scope here. TattooPerson can draw from either _images or _altImages
  depending on which walk sequence is active (see Sprite::_altImages), so
  queueRoseTattooHiresPersonOverride() tries both in turn.

- widget_inventory.cpp's existing queueRoseTattooHiresSprite() call is
  updated for the new signature (passes its ImageFrame through).

Override assets are generated the same way as existing cursor/inventory
overrides (see tools/upscale_rosetattoo_sprites.py --mode photo), at
"sprites/<resourceName>/frame_NNN@Sx.png", keyed by resourceName (derived
from e.g. "WATSON.VGS" -> "watson_vgs") and frame index - no new asset
naming convention or extraction step was needed.

Depends on rosetattoo-hires-cursor-ai-override.patch (for
loadRoseTattooHiresCursorOverride(), the underlying override-asset loader
this reuses).

